e you can see  um it's a little bit tough but uh uh in  the middle uh you can see a bi-colored  damselfish we use those for tumor  studies um unfortunately we got to keep  them separated because they're highly  aggressive with each other but uh  they're used for tumor studies and  they're in a traditional uh  recirculating system this is a our brand  new facility and and uh you can see  there's many many many of them in here  and all this water is recirculated  another fish that we've used here at the  university of miami is the photos from  my friend dr lou  this is the jade gobi and these are used  a lot for auditory research  i used to inspect his facilities a lot  and i fell in love with the fish so much  that i have one in my own tank and uh  the one in my own tank is about four  times the size of the ones that you see  on the left so they're really really  groovy fish uh and they're and they're  fun to watch they have unique behavior  but they're using auditory research and  most people wouldn't think  that fishes are using auditory research  so just a moment i'm going to show you a  a young mahi that's in a swim chamber  in a swim chamber  can measure physiologic responses we can  count opercular movements and that can  give us some estimation of o2  consumption  and it's oftentimes related with images  that are captured by a high-speed  computer  and we can do analyses later  we can change the water from quick to  slow um we can change temperature  salinity all kinds of parameters and see  the effect or we can expose them to to  potential pollution or potential toxins  things like that  and uh the way we manage it is that we  kind of make it analogous to using a  treadmill in mice and rats so it's the  same sort of idea so you'll see this  little mahi swimming has swimming his  tail off so to speak  go ahead  so like i said that was a quick video  but you can see uh we can we can see uh  that animal and we can do a lot of a lot  of analyses uh with high-speed computers  um  we also are the university of miami is a  national resource center for applesia uh  apresia  is a  the california sea hare and they're used  a lot in neuroscience and we are the  nih's national center for research  resources with this species  here's some pictures of our setup on the  upper left is uh an adult  plegia californica  they can get to be about the size of a  football  so they're quite impressive when they're  big  and you can see in the other pictures  are set ups and  of course there are there there are no  real commercial setups for these so some  of you may recognize the polycarbonate  rat cages that are used uh in the in the  pictures so we're doing internal  recycling  of our rat cages to facilitate this  project um and uh on the bottom right as  you can see that's where we're growing  the the babies out from the eggs so it's  a pretty cool setup and uh we're real  happy about that real proud of that  facility  uh and the reason that we're so good at  it is because we've got sun and we've  got seaweed  so we can do this better than anybody  and that's just a picture of a vats that  are containing nothing but seaweed that  are a pleasure to eat like it's candy  so wrapping up some resources that are  available to you  from a diagnostic sense um i i've  utilized the university of florida um  usda athes  has a lot of information for aquaculture  if you're not in florida you know  contact your agricultural college  extension offices that's what they're  there for there's also professional  internet fish veterinary groups like uh  association of fish veterinarians  and when in doubt contact your local  veterinary school and they can help  point you in the right direction if you  want to do some some advanced  diagnostics maybe some pcr some virology  things like that  so take away points are you can do it um  it doesn't uh it doesn't take an expert  you can become an expert very quickly  and uh you know as long as you plan  ahead of time before you study and know  your species  you're really ahead of the game  and our aquatic systems share a lot of  the same similarities between marine and  freshwater facilities so  you may have a lot of experience with  you know zebrafish third time i've said  it  and if so that's fine you know you can  easily use a lot of that same experience  and same knowledge base to do pretty  much any marine species so long as you  do your homework beforehand  but just remember that there's often  sorry there are often little data on  most of our non-traditional species and  sometimes the pi is the expert so it's  it's important that we work together  with them and have a real positive  relationship with them um so just some  some quick acknowledgements um thanks to  dr julia zayas my director here at the  division of veterinary resources dr dan  binetti he's a director of aquaculture  here at the university of miami  lots of aquaculture folks especially ron  and john  have been just totally awesome and  helped me out a lot and my mentor dr roy  yannong  at the tropical aquaculture laboratory  at the university of florida um in that  picture you can see those are we call  them the a team that's our aquaculture  team and we're quite proud of their  accomplishments um and uh the they're  just an outstanding group of individuals  so thank you very much um i appreciate  your time we'll take some questions in a  second and uh again this is a a picture  of uh the keys in february so sorry  about uh everybody who's not  or who's battling the snow i should say  so i think  thank you very much dan for that great  presentation before we get started on  the question and answer session i would  like to remind our audience how to  submit questions  you can submit questions by typing them  in the q a box which can be found by  clicking on the green q a button at the  lower left of the presentation window  and we'll try to answer as many  questions as we can  our first question  we have from the audience  is i know this might not be related but  do you know if there is a strategy to  exterminate the lionfish i understand it  is quite invasive and it is negatively  impacting the fishing industry  um there there's lots of different uh  ideas out there um  you know but really uh at this point i  think it's uh it's just manual removal i  think that a lot of people are very  nervous and very scared because uh in  the atlantic this is an apex predator  and um they're  they're they're eating lots of reef  fishes and i don't think uh that  the the field really understands um  everything about how the lionfish has  adapted to the the atlantic you know  they're from the pacific and it's a  colder water species but they seem to  have tolerated um being here but their  ability to tolerate cold water allows  them to go a lot deeper so  i'm not sure that there's a real good  plan out there and i think that the  wildlife folks are really nervous about  it to be honest with you  great we do have another question at  what point do you think it would be  appropriate to grow your own live feed  for adult animals it would eliminate  your concerns for biosecurity  um  i would say that uh you know we grow our  own foods for for obviously for the  juveniles i i don't think it would be  particularly cost effective for um the  large fishes um  it could be done um but it would be it  would be very labor intensive and most  people don't have the space to grow out  the sheer numbers that we would we would  need um you know in our case so i think  you're really kind of uh you're kind of  stuck with these uh bait producers but  um  you know if if chance you found the  disease outbreak or or you were  concerned about that  or if you know you had a smaller  population of animals that's totally  reasonable to do um but if you're going  to be feeding live fish to other fish  that's something that your iacook needs  to know about and needs to make some  decisions on  because  getting eaten alive i think would be  potentially distressful  okay our next question if you have a  large sick fish and you collect a blood  sample for analysis do you have to  process that sample yourself or can you  send it to a contract lab  um you can send it to a contract lab i  mean you can do some processing yourself  it really depends on on you know your  facilities i mean i'm at a medical  school so i have a little bit more  options in other places but you could  certainly um collect blood and then and  then ship it you know the key is is to  talk to the laboratories beforehand uh  and and have some communication with  them for exactly what their capabilities  are and what they what they would want  but certainly you can do your own um  processing as well um but depending on  what your institution's capabilities are  for the most part um you know medical  schools don't have a lot of the the pcr  uh primers and things that you would  need for for fishes but um if you're at  a veterinary school or something like  that then you may have an internal  laboratory that would work but there are  a lot of third-party laboratories um  that can really help you out in that  situation so um it kind of depends on on  what you're doing  and where you're at  okay and we have our last question you  refer to biosecurity a lot with the  animals do you believe this is equally  important when it comes to selecting  your water source  yes and no  i certainly  i would prefer if i could make my own  water but um you know it's definitely a  concern um we we have had outbreaks of  uh external parasites before from  incoming water um that's happened on a  couple of occasions but for the most  part we don't have that issue  you know it is a potential risk but it's  something that we monitor regularly for  and if we were to have an outbreak of a  particularly viral disease or something  like that we would be easily be able to  isolate  the affected animals and then  test them and potentially  eradicate that pathogen but certainly  it's uh it's a concern  you know my biggest concern is is  external parasites because they're  they're so prevalent and uh the other  thing you know it's not quite bio  security but with incoming water is  um  you know there could be a toxin spill  you saw the the slide where the  collection is out in the bay uh there  could be some yahoo that decides to dump  uh  oil or or gasoline out of his boat right  there um and then that could potentially  get to our fishes but um  that's that's the things that worry me  the most so um biosecurity a little bit  but you know it's kind of difficult when  you're pushing you know hundreds of  thousands of gallons a day so  it can be a bit of a challenge  thank you dr rovin unfortunately we are  out of time i want to thank the audience  for their interesting questions and  their participation in today's event and  thank you dr rothen for all of your um  excellent videos and pictures today's  webcast will be available for on-demand  viewing through august 2015 and you'll  receive an email from lab roots alerting  you to when this webcast will be  available for replay we invite you to  pass this announcement along to your  colleagues who may have missed today's  live event  that's all for now goodbye and see you  next time
 

